; 14-sandbox.kurt

; `sandbox` opens a scratch block: whatever you do inside is thrown away once
; the block closes, and never becomes part of the surrounding theory.  It's
; the place to try out a derivation without committing to it.

bool A
use A

sandbox
    use false        ; ...anything goes in here, even something silly
    A
    false

; dedenting out of the sandbox closes it, exactly like closing any other
; block -- just with nothing derived from it, everything inside is discarded:

A                   ; still fine, `A` was a real axiom, from outside the sandbox
; false             ; would fail: `false` only ever existed inside the sandbox

; `break` (25-break.kurt) closes a sandbox (or any block) immediately,
; without needing to write something at a shallower indentation first --
; handy when you already know you want to abandon it right now.
